API layer
=========

Everything that crosses the network lives in src/api/. No page imports axios.


Files
-----

	api/client.ts                 the axios instance, tokenStorage, interceptors
	api/socket.ts                 the single Socket.IO connection
	api/challenges/challengeApi.ts    challenges, participants, approvals
	api/challenges/challengeTypes.ts
	api/quest/questApi.ts             quest bank and daily rotation
	api/quest/questTypes.ts
	api/activities/activityApi.ts     activity catalogue
	api/activities/activityType.ts
	api/leaderboard/leaderboardApi.ts faculty and campus boards

Domain folders each hold the calls and the response types for one area of the
backend. A page imports the function and the type, never the URL.


Base URL
--------

	const api = axios.create({
	  baseURL: import.meta.env.VITE_API_PATH || "http://localhost:8000/api",
	});

The backend mounts every route under /api. VITE_API_PATH must therefore
include it. Omitting it makes every request, including login, return 404.

api/socket.ts derives the socket origin from the same value by stripping a
trailing /api. One variable configures both.


Tokens
------

tokenStorage in client.ts is the only code that touches localStorage:

	getAccessToken, getRefreshToken, setTokens, clearTokens

A request interceptor attaches Authorization: Bearer <access token> to every
outgoing call.


Refresh
-------

The response interceptor implements single-flight refresh:

1. A 401 on any request other than /auth/refresh marks the request for retry.
2. If a refresh is already in progress the request is pushed onto a queue and
   replayed when that refresh settles. Only one refresh call is ever in
   flight.
3. If the refresh succeeds the original request is retried once with the new
   token.
4. If the refresh itself 401s, tokens are cleared and a window event
   auth:logout is dispatched. AuthContext listens for it and sends the user
   back to /login.

Step 4 is why a genuinely expired session does not recurse: the refresh URL is
checked before the retry logic runs.


Adding a call
-------------

1. Put the response shape in the domain's Types file. Match the backend
   serializer field for field; if the serializer sends facultyKey and level,
   the type carries them.
2. Add an exported async function to the domain's Api file. Return the parsed
   payload, not the axios response.
3. Import it in the page. Load in an effect, set local state.
4. If another admin or a student can change what you just fetched, subscribe
   to the matching socket event and call the same loader again.


Socket usage
------------

	connectSocket()     called once by AdminLayout after login
	getSocket()         used by pages to attach listeners
	disconnectSocket()  called on sign out

A page attaches listeners in an effect and must remove them in the cleanup
return. Forgetting the cleanup leaves handlers bound to unmounted components
and produces a slow leak plus duplicated refetches on every navigation.

	useEffect(() => {
	  const socket = getSocket();
	  if (!socket) return;
	  socket.on("validation:resolved", refresh);
	  return () => { socket.off("validation:resolved", refresh); };
	}, [refresh]);

The refresh callback should be wrapped in useCallback so the effect does not
rebind on every render.


Error handling
--------------

There is no global error toast. Pages surface failures inline where the action
was taken. context/ToastContext.tsx exists but is not wired into the tree; see
docs/maintenance.md before adopting it.